      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Method"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "orderedList",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Combine all ingredients except the chicken in a bowl. Mix well. Add the chicken thighs and coat thoroughly. Cover and refrigerate for at least 1 hour, or overnight."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Remove the chicken from the fridge 30 minutes before cooking. Preheat the oven to 170C."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Place the spring onions in a layer on the bottom of the dutch oven. Arrange the chicken thighs on top, skin-side up. Pour over all the marinade."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Add 100ml of water. Seal the dutch oven with its lid. Place in the oven."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Braise for 1 hour. Remove the lid and increase the oven to 200C. Cook for a further 20 minutes to caramelise the skin and reduce the sauce."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Remove the whole spices. Serve the chicken with rice, spooning the glossy sauce generously over the top."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "troubleshooter",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "troubleshooterItem",
            "attrs": {
              "symptom": "Sauce burns on the bottom of the pot",
              "cause": "The oven temperature was too high or there was too little liquid.",
              "fix": "Add 50ml of water and reduce the oven temperature to 160C. Check again after 20 minutes."
            }
          },
          {
            "type": "troubleshooterItem",
            "attrs": {
              "symptom": "Skin does not colour during the final stage",
              "cause": "Too much liquid remained after removing the lid.",
              "fix": "Transfer the chicken to a baking tray and place under the grill for 5 minutes to finish the skin."
            }
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Where this dish lives"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Beggar's chicken is one of the most theatrical dishes in Chinese restaurant culture. In Hangzhou restaurants, it still arrives at the table encased in a clay shell. A waiter breaks it open with a mallet, releasing a cloud of fragrant steam. The story of its origin, set during the Qing dynasty, has many versions, but all involve a clever improvisation with fire and mud. It is now listed as one of the traditional dishes of Zhejiang province."
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
